WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
Ethene is a gas.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.960
Join thousands of ethene molecules end to end,

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.840
and you get polyethylene, a solid tough enough

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.360
for bags and milk bottles.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.160
Swap one hydrogen on each ethene for a

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.480
chlorine, and you get the plastic in grey

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.280
PVC water pipes.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.720
So how does an alkene turn into a

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.360
solid like this, and why do plastics made

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.320
from such similar molecules behave so differently?

00:00:26.334 --> 00:00:28.493
A small molecule that joins up into a

00:00:28.574 --> 00:00:31.293
long chain is a monomer, and the chain

00:00:31.374 --> 00:00:34.093
is a polymer, meaning many parts.

00:00:34.414 --> 00:00:37.453
Remember, an alkene's double bond has a weaker

00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:38.813
second bond.

00:00:38.894 --> 00:00:42.893
In addition polymerisation, that second bond breaks, leaving

00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:45.373
each carbon with one spare bond.

00:00:45.694 --> 00:00:48.173
One spare bond links to the chain behind,

00:00:48.254 --> 00:00:50.173
and the other reaches out to the next

00:00:50.254 --> 00:00:50.653
monomer.

00:00:51.294 --> 00:00:54.573
A starter molecule called an initiator opens the

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:57.293
first double bond, and each new chain end

00:00:57.374 --> 00:01:00.013
opens the next one, thousands of times.

00:01:00.494 --> 00:01:01.613
Nothing else is made.

00:01:02.094 --> 00:01:04.573
Every atom of every monomer ends up in

00:01:04.614 --> 00:01:05.293
the chain.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.420
You can't draw thousands of units, so you

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.060
draw the part that repeats, once.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.580
Take chloroethene.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.900
Rewrite it with the double bond as a

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.380
single bond, and draw a bond sticking out

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.500
from each of the two carbons, called a

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.900
continuation bond, to show the chain carries on.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.540
Put square brackets around it, with those bonds

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:30.900
poking through, and a small n outside, meaning

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.020
a large number.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.020
That's PVC.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.740
The two carbons from the double bond form

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.100
the backbone, and everything else, here the chlorine,

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.300
hangs off the side.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.620
To find the monomer from a repeat unit,

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.700
go backwards.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:50.100
Take the two backbone carbons inside the brackets,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.340
remove the two bonds that poke out, and

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.500
put the double bond back between them.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.140
Don't add hydrogens where those bonds were.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.180
Once the double bond is back, each carbon

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.100
already has its four bonds.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.500
So polystyrene's repeat unit gives styrene, an ethene

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.580
with a benzene ring, a ring of six

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.660
carbons, on one carbon.

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:15.706
So why is polyethylene a solid, when ethene

00:02:15.787 --> 00:02:16.786
is a gas?

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:19.946
Each chain is huge, and it lies alongside

00:02:20.027 --> 00:02:22.386
its neighbours for thousands of carbons.

00:02:22.587 --> 00:02:25.786
Dispersion forces act all along that contact, so

00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:28.306
together they hold the chains firmly.

00:02:28.507 --> 00:02:31.226
No bonds join one chain to another, only

00:02:31.347 --> 00:02:32.346
these forces.

00:02:32.587 --> 00:02:35.146
Warm the plastic, and the chains move faster,

00:02:35.267 --> 00:02:37.386
until they can slide past each other, so

00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:39.266
it softens and can be moulded.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:41.626
Cool it, and the forces hold the chains

00:02:41.707 --> 00:02:42.226
again.

00:02:42.427 --> 00:02:45.626
The covalent bonds inside each chain don't break.

00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:48.426
A plastic that softens like this, again and

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:51.626
again, is a thermoplastic, and that's what lets

00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:53.706
it be melted down and recycled.

00:02:55.734 --> 00:02:58.933
Polyethylene comes in two main forms, made under

00:02:59.014 --> 00:03:00.293
different conditions.

00:03:00.614 --> 00:03:05.173
Low density polyethylene, LDPE, has short branches

00:03:05.214 --> 00:03:06.813
sticking out of its chains.

00:03:07.094 --> 00:03:09.893
The branches stop the chains lying close together,

00:03:09.974 --> 00:03:13.013
so there's less contact, and weaker dispersion forces

00:03:13.214 --> 00:03:13.933
between them.

00:03:14.214 --> 00:03:16.933
It's softer and less dense, and it melts

00:03:16.974 --> 00:03:19.093
at a lower temperature, about a hundred and

00:03:19.174 --> 00:03:19.893
ten degrees.

00:03:20.454 --> 00:03:23.453
That suits squeeze bottles and thin films.

00:03:23.894 --> 00:03:28.573
High density polyethylene, HDPE, has long straight chains

00:03:28.694 --> 00:03:32.253
that pack closely, with regions lined up neatly.

00:03:32.374 --> 00:03:35.693
More contact means stronger forces, so it's denser

00:03:35.734 --> 00:03:38.253
and stiffer, and it melts nearer a hundred

00:03:38.374 --> 00:03:39.813
and thirty degrees.

00:03:39.894 --> 00:03:41.973
That suits crates and milk bottles.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.520
Now change the monomer.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.680
In PVC, every second carbon carries a chlorine,

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.480
and the carbon chlorine bond is polar.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.440
So neighbouring chains also attract each other by

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.240
dipole-dipole forces, on top of dispersion.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.840
The chains grip each other harder, which makes

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.280
plain PVC rigid, good for water pipes.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.720
Mix in small molecules called plasticisers, and they

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.520
push the chains apart, so the PVC turns

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.400
flexible, like the coating on electrical cables.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.519
Polystyrene has a bulky benzene ring on every

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.639
second carbon.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.040
You might expect that to make it soft,

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.880
like LDPE's branches.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.080
But these rings are big and stiff, and

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.720
they catch on the rings of neighbouring chains,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.200
so at room temperature the chains can hardly

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.480
move or slide.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.400
That makes solid polystyrene rigid, and it snaps

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:39.600
rather than bends.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.240
Blow gas into it as it sets, and

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.400
you get a foam, light and full of

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.720
trapped air, so it's a good insulator for

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:47.840
hot drink cups.

00:04:49.867 --> 00:04:54.026
Finally, swap every hydrogen in ethene for fluorine,

00:04:54.107 --> 00:04:56.586
and polymerise tetrafluoroethene.

00:04:57.067 --> 00:04:59.146
You get PTFE.

00:05:00.107 --> 00:05:02.826
Carbon fluorine bonds are among the strongest single

00:05:02.907 --> 00:05:05.786
bonds carbon makes, and the fluorine atoms wrap

00:05:05.867 --> 00:05:08.906
closely around the backbone, so very few chemicals

00:05:08.987 --> 00:05:10.266
can attack it.

00:05:10.347 --> 00:05:12.426
The C F bonds are polar, but the

00:05:12.507 --> 00:05:15.626
fluorines surround the chain evenly, so their pulls

00:05:15.747 --> 00:05:17.066
largely cancel.

00:05:17.147 --> 00:05:19.786
And fluorine holds its electrons so tightly that

00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:23.146
they hardly shift, so other substances form only

00:05:23.307 --> 00:05:26.266
very weak dispersion forces with the surface.

00:05:26.347 --> 00:05:28.506
So almost nothing sticks to it.

00:05:28.587 --> 00:05:30.906
That's why it coats non-stick pans, and makes

00:05:30.987 --> 00:05:31.786
plumber's tape.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.680
So, to draw an addition polymer, open the

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.280
double bond and add brackets, and remember, nothing

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.200
is lost.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.200
To explain a property, trace it back.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.840
The branching or the side group sets how

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.480
closely the chains pack, and how hard they

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.800
pull on each other, and that decides the

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:52.840
use.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.440
PTFE's resistance to chemicals is the exception: that

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.760
comes from its strong C F bonds.

00:06:00.767 --> 00:06:01.406
Your turn.

00:06:01.487 --> 00:06:05.886
1,1-dichloroethene forms an addition polymer.

00:06:06.287 --> 00:06:07.846
Draw its repeat unit.

00:06:08.127 --> 00:06:11.086
Then, another addition polymer has the repeat unit

00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:11.646
shown.

00:06:12.127 --> 00:06:13.926
Name and draw its monomer.

00:06:14.207 --> 00:06:15.486
Pause here to answer.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.340
Open the double bond, keep both chlorines on

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:32.540
the same carbon, and add the continuation bonds,

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:33.900
the brackets and the n.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.540
For the second one, put the double bond

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.780
back between the backbone carbons.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:41.260
That's propene, with the methyl group hanging off

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.300
the side.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:45.180
Adding hydrogens where the continuation bonds were is

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:46.220
the trap.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.020
That gives propane, which can't polymerise.

00:06:51.034 --> 00:06:51.673
Last one.

00:06:52.314 --> 00:06:56.233
HDPE is used for rigid chopping boards, and

00:06:56.474 --> 00:07:00.233
LDPE for soft squeeze bottles, and both are

00:07:00.314 --> 00:07:01.273
made from ethene.

00:07:01.994 --> 00:07:04.793
Explain how differences in their chain structure cause

00:07:04.874 --> 00:07:07.593
these properties, and why both can be melted

00:07:07.674 --> 00:07:09.353
and remoulded for recycling.

00:07:09.914 --> 00:07:11.273
Pause here to answer.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:26.580
HDPE's straight chains pack closely, giving more contact

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.620
and stronger dispersion forces, so it's stiff.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:33.220
LDPE's branches keep its chains apart, so the

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.500
forces are weaker, and it's softer.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.260
No bonds join one chain to another, so

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.500
heating lets the chains slide, and they can

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:41.380
be remoulded.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:44.340
Saying melting breaks the bonds in the chains

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:45.220
is the trap.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.340
Only the forces between the chains are overcome.

00:07:50.367 --> 00:07:54.686
Next: polymers that give something off, usually water,

00:07:54.767 --> 00:07:56.926
every time two monomers join.

00:07:58.934 --> 00:08:00.373
Here are the key notes.

00:08:00.614 --> 00:08:02.533
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:02.614 --> 00:08:03.413
to copy them.
